<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khusobek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sobekkhu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khusobek, also called Sobekkhu, was an Egyptian army officer of the Twelfth Dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>. His stele from Abydos records campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and an expedition by Senusret III to a place called Sekmem, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>. It is the main Egyptian evidence for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> military campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p></div>
